afety supervision. In a study by Putri et al. [146], an E-nose based on supervised machine learning (LDA, quadratic discriminant analysis (QDA), K-NN, and RF) was used to classify meat floss derived from beef, chicken, and pork. The LDA model with five windows achieved an accuracy of over 99% in distinguishing the three types of meat floss, and the analysis results of the E-nose were validated by spectral data. These experiments highlight the effectiveness of pattern recognition methods as a powerful analytical tool for identifying meat adulteration and evaluating freshness using pattern recognition method. Qian et al. [147] proposed a hybrid feature optimization method, which uses the mutual information hybrid evaluation


